{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>You shall not take a wife for my son from the daughters of the Canaanite among whom I dwell.</b> But do we not yet know that he dwells among them? Rather, he comes to resolve a difficulty by this. For presumably he commanded that he not intermarry with the daughters of the Canaanite lest he learn from their deeds, in the manner stated in the Torah (Deuteronomy 7:3): ״וְלֹא תִתְחַתֵּן בָּם״ etc., ״כִּי יָסִיר אֶת בִּנְךָ מֵאַחֲרַי״ etc. If so, what would he gain if he marries a wife from the daughters of Laban and Bethuel, for they too are idolaters like the Canaanite? Therefore he said 'among whom I dwell,' and he said 'lest you bring back my son there' — for he said: it is obvious, if my son marries a wife from the daughters of the Canaanite, since I dwell among them, presumably my son too will sojourn among them, and then there is reason to fear lest he learn from their deeds. And if he marries from the daughters of Laban and Bethuel and dwells among them, then there is likewise reason to fear lest he learn from their deeds. But when he marries a wife from there and dwels here, then whichever way you look at it there is no room for concern: from Laban and Bethuel he will not learn, for he will not dwell among them; and from the Canaanite he will not learn, for he will have no intermingling with them — and on the contrary, they will hate him because he did not wish to intermarry with them. However, there is another concern, and that is that the nature of the fathers extends also to the sons — and this is specifically regarding those sins that come from the side of matter, such as eating and lust and gluttony and jealousy and all the bad traits that depend on matter. These blemishes spread from fathers to sons, and their consequences likewise. But idolatry, which depends on a person's intellect, does not spread from fathers to sons, for there are three partners in a person: the matter comes from father and mother; therefore all deeds of the fathers that depend on matter are inherited by the sons. But faith depends on a person's intellect, and the Holy One, blessed be He, gives him a soul and intellect — why should this spread from fathers to sons? Therefore he distanced the Canaanites, who were steeped in lust and many kinds of sins that depend on a person's matter aside from idolatry, and he did not distance Laban and Bethuel, in whom there was nothing but the deficiency of idolatry alone." "Another matter: therefore he said 'among whom I dwell' — meaning, in the fabric of their heart I sit, and all their deeds and thoughts come before me, for perhaps by the sight of the eyes you would judge, for there are among them hypocrites who do the deed of Zimri and seek reward like Phinehas, and you would think that they are fit and worthy of intermarrying with them. Therefore he said: from my own flesh I behold, through the holy spirit that is upon me, that there is not one among them who does good — for I am as though I sit within their heart and know their inward part as well as their outward. And therefore Eliezer changed it and said 'in whose land I dwell,' so that Laban and Bethuel should not say: 'If so, he is a prophet and searches hearts, and we are deceivers, but our deception will not avail us, for he knows everything that is in our heart' — and perhaps on this account they would refrain from intermarrying with him. Therefore he changed and said that Abraham said 'in whose land I dwell.'" "And there are those who say that he said 'among whom I dwell' to exclude also the daughters of Aner, Eshcol, and Mamre, among whom he dwells, for after all they are of cursed seed. And Eliezer thought that if he said thus, their mind would be provoked, for they would say: 'He is compelled to intermarry with us, for he has no way to turn right or left,' and they would force him to come to them. Therefore he said 'in whose land I dwell,' that is, unspecified Canaanites, but those among whom he dwells — it is permitted to him to intermarry with them; through this their mind would not be provoked against them. And this is what he said: ״וְאֶפְנֶה עַל יָמִין אוֹ עַל שְׂמֹאל״ (Genesis 24:49), to indicate that there is right and left before him. But the truth is that he did not permit him from the daughters of Aner, Eshcol, and Mamre, and thus the Ramban explained."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:39.417066767Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}